{"cases":[{"attack":true,"case":"The proposed evidence ('Cross-Substrate Invariance') is a necessary but insufficient condition for true independence. The target's own countercondition admits a non-substrate shared cause (a logic bug in a common library used across all substrates). Under that countercondition, failures could perfectly satisfy cross-substrate invariance—different substrates are unaffected by the substrate-based partition—yet remain highly correlated due to the shared library bug. Thus 'Cross-Substrate Invariance' cannot distinguish truly independent failures from pseudo-uncorrelated ones in exactly the class of cases the problem asks about.","counterexample":[{"scenario":"Three services A,B,C on three different substrates all use the same open-source TLS library; a latent bug causes each to crash independently-looking at staggered times; cross-substrate invariance holds, but failures are correlated by the shared library bug, not independent."}],"target":"44449c528866cc31fa57ec8a1e66c3171b211a856133ec4059d3a79eebdf1b94"},{"attack":true,"case":"The 'Redundancy Paradox' mechanism asserts that adding identical nodes increases the probability a latent coordination bug triggers a simultaneous global crash, but this conflates redundancy with monoculture. Strictly more internal redundancy need not be identical-node redundancy; a monolith can add diverse nodes, hot/warm spares, or redundant independent implementations internally. Therefore the argument does not engage the problem's premise that the large service has 'strictly more total internal redundancy'—it attacks a narrower case (identical-node scaling) and leaves the general claim untouched.","counterexample":[{"scenario":"A large service adds 10 redundant execution paths using three different codebases and two independent schedulers; total internal redundancy strictly increases while systemic-bug correlated-failure risk decreases, contradicting the claimed paradox."}],"target":"579b7444be27c17903965a9217ecf7a70135f1991a5f5ca4abd8e303a8a324e8"},{"attack":true,"case":"The statistical signature proposed is not discriminating. The target's own countercondition states that a hidden shared trigger occurring at a frequency/distribution mimicking a Poisson process would evade the test. But more deeply, even burstiness is ambiguous: truly independent failures can exhibit apparent clustering purely by chance in finite samples (Poisson processes produce runs), while a hidden shared cause with staggered propagation delays can produce a non-bursty, evenly spread failure pattern. Hence temporal burstiness/latency cannot reliably distinguish true independence from pseudo-uncorrelated failure.","counterexample":[{"scenario":"Independent services fail at constant rate lambda; in a finite 30-day window their inter-arrival times show apparent clustering (a 3-failure burst) by chance alone; the method misclassifies this as a hidden shared cause."}],"target":"ebcad9f88fafbd1ba336193d86badb9df8dc4cba5337506e4675d3e661f7318b"},{"attack":true,"case":"The mechanism assumes decentralized networks require complex coordination protocols, but the target's own countercondition concedes they may use a sufficiently simple or asynchronous coordination model. In that conceded case, the 'coordination tax' largely disappears, so the central claim that the large service is more resilient 'because' it operates above a correlation horizon of coordination complexity no longer holds—removing the asserted advantage without establishing any separate reason the monolith is more resilient. The argument thus fails to establish the claim for the very network design it acknowledges as possible.","counterexample":[{"scenario":"A decentralized network uses asynchronous, eventually-consistent gossip with no consensus; coordination-driven correlated failure is negligible, so the monolith's avoidance of a 'coordination tax' confers no resilience advantage."}],"target":"f572887161d13414560f5884a569a4b306cb6b31bedd882b5f18238cb797c47f"}]}